.TH LD 1 "July 5, 2026" "ReBSD"
.SH NAME
ld \- ReBSD a.out and ELF32 link editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ld
[
.B \-\-elf
|
.B \-\-aout
]
[
.B \-m
.I emulation
]
[
.B \-sSxXrdtv
]
[
.B \-EL
|
.B \-EB
]
[
.B \-\-sysroot
.I dir
|
.BI \-\-sysroot= dir
]
[
.B \-L
.I dir
]
[
.B \-o
.I file
]
[
.B \-l
.I name
]
[
.B \-u
.I name
]
[
.B \-e
.I name
]
[
.B \-T
.I address-or-script
]
.I file
\&...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ld
combines ReBSD
.B a.out
or ELF32 object files and archives into an executable or relocatable output
file.  ELF inputs may target MIPS32 or 32-bit i386; objects for different
machines or byte orders cannot be mixed.
.PP
The default output file is
.IR a.out .
Archive libraries are searched at the point where they appear on the
command line.
Libraries indexed by
.BR ranlib (1)
contain a
.B __.SYMDEF
member that lets
.B ld
load archive members needed to satisfy unresolved references.
.PP
The linker defines the standard end symbols
.BR _etext ,
.BR _edata ,
.BR _end ,
and their C names
.BR etext ,
.BR edata ,
and
.BR end .
It also defines the constructor and destructor range symbols used by the
ReBSD C runtime.
Input text/data/BSS alignment is preserved in the output.  For MIPS this
keeps VR4300 8-byte text alignment and Malta MIPS32r2 4-byte text
alignment compatible with the CPU mode selected by
.BR as (1)
and
.BR pcc (1).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \--elf
Select ELF32 linking.  ELF input also selects this mode automatically.
.TP
.B \--aout
Select the legacy ReBSD a.out linker.
.TP
.BI \-m " emulation"
Select
.B elf_i386
or an ELF32 MIPS emulation.  The machine is otherwise taken from the first
ELF object.
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Write the output to
.IR file .
.TP
.BI \-L " dir"
Add
.I dir
to the library search path.
All
.B \-L
directories are collected before any
.B \-l
library is resolved, so compiler drivers may place default library
directories after user objects.
.TP
.BI \-\-sysroot " dir"
.TQ
.BI \-\-sysroot= dir
Use
.I dir
as the root for standard library directories.
The default sysroot is
.IR / .
Explicit
.B \-L
directories are used as written.
.TP
.BI \-l " name"
Search for
.BI lib name .a
in the library search path.
The standard library directories are
.IR /lib ,
.IR /usr/lib ,
and
.IR /usr/local/lib ,
under the selected sysroot.
Soft-float PCC links normally add
.I /usr/lib/softfloat
before
.I /usr/lib
so compiler helper routines are selected from the soft-float
.BR libpcc.a .
.TP
.BI \-u " symbol"
Enter
.I symbol
as an initially undefined symbol.
This is useful when linking only from libraries.
.TP
.BI \-e " symbol"
Use
.I symbol
as the output entry point.
If no entry point is specified, the target default text base is used.
.TP
.BI \-T " address-or-script"
Set the a.out text base address, or read an ELF linker script.
.TP
.B \-EL
Read and write little\-endian objects.
.TP
.B \-EB
Read and write big\-endian objects.  ELF32/i386 rejects this option.
.TP
.B \-r
Produce relocatable output and preserve relocation records.
Undefined symbols are allowed in relocatable output.
.TP
.B \-d
Force common symbols to be allocated even when relocatable output is
requested.
.TP
.B \-s
Strip all symbols from final output.
.TP
.B \-S
Discard non\-loadable absolute and debug\-like symbols while retaining the
symbols needed by normal toolchain output.
.TP
.B \-x
Discard local symbols.
.TP
.B \-X
Discard local symbols whose names start with
.B L
or
.BR . .
.TP
.B \-t
Increase trace verbosity.
This option may be repeated for more detailed linker diagnostics.
.TP
.B \-v
Enable verbose linker diagnostics.
.TP
.B \-\-fatal\-warnings
Accepted for compiler\-driver compatibility.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I a.out
Default output file.
.TP
.I /lib/lib*.a
Standard libraries.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/lib*.a
Standard libraries.
.TP
.I /usr/local/lib/lib*.a
Standard libraries.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/ldscripts/elf32-i386.ld
Default ELF32/i386 linker script.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR as (1),
.BR cc (1),
.BR pcc (1),
.BR ranlib (1),
.BR nm (1),
.BR size (1),
.BR a.out (5)
